<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zain-ul-Abidin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1395">1395</a>–<a href="/years/1470">1470</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Zain al-Abidin of Kashmir, Budshah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zain-ul-Abidin, called Budshah, the great king, was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> from 1418 to 1470 in the Shah Mir dynasty. He reversed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a>'s persecution, allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Pandits to return, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works translated into Persian and patronised crafts. He built canals, bridges and towns, and is remembered as the greatest ruler of the sultanate.</p></div>
